========================================================
HEADER vs HEADERS – REST ASSURED
========================================================

1. Header
-----------
Header represents a SINGLE HTTP header.

Class:
import io.restassured.http.Header;

Example:
Header header = new Header("Content-Type", "application/json");

Syntax:
Header headerName = new Header("Header-Name", "Header-Value");


2. Headers
-----------
Headers represents MULTIPLE HTTP headers (a collection of Header objects).

Class:
import io.restassured.http.Headers;

Example:
Headers headers = new Headers(
        new Header("Content-Type", "application/json"),
        new Header("Authorization", "Bearer token")
);


3. Getting Response Headers
----------------------------
To get all response headers:

Headers headers = rs.headers();

To get a specific header value:

String contentType = rs.header("Content-Type");

OR

String contentType = headers.getValue("Content-Type");


4. Common Header Assertions
----------------------------
Verify header exists:

Assert.assertNotNull(rs.header("Content-Type"));


Verify exact header value:

Assert.assertEquals(
        rs.header("Content-Type"),
        "application/json"
);


Verify header contains a value:

Assert.assertTrue(
        rs.header("Content-Type").contains("application/json")
);


5. Important Headers to Validate
---------------------------------
- Content-Type
- Content-Length (when required)
- Cache-Control (when required)
- Authorization (request header)
- Security headers (when required)

========================================================
EASY REMEMBER
========================================================

Header  → ONE header
Headers → MULTIPLE headers / collection

Example:

Header  → Content-Type = application/json

Headers → Content-Type + Authorization + Accept + etc.
